Host sidecar regression tests
============================

Absent forks
------------

Host catalogs accept resource/metadata-only files in ._name, %name,
.AppleDouble/name and .finf/.rsrc layouts. Indexed enumeration and directory
counts include these files once, using a valid companion as their catalog
entry. Invalid/truncated AppleDouble files do not become orphan catalog entries.
Opening an absent fork supplies empty read/seek semantics without creating its
named host file. A nonzero write, SetEOF or allocation materializes it, preserving
independent marks on other open references. Renaming/deleting an unmaterialized
open fork returns fBsyErr; close it first.

New files initially have only an empty data fork. Creating a file with no requested
Finder type or creator uses the host entry's timestamps and creates no metadata
sidecar. Explicit metadata or resource writes create their companions when needed.
Existing resource-only files remain supported; when their data is first written,
a proven empty metadata anchor may be retired. Nonempty FinderInfo, dates, unknown
descriptors, ATTR data and resources are retained. Reading/opening ordinary data
files does not create empty resource sidecars. Existing files are not swept or
bulk converted.

The focused Windows tests in the Existing coverage section include absent forks and first-write transitions.
For an actual staged File Manager lifecycle test, generate a disposable GDB probe:

  python system/tests/create-empty-fork-probe.py BUILD_DIRECTORY APPLICATION
  gdb -batch -x BUILD_DIRECTORY/probe.gdb --args windows-binaries/bin/ROMAW.exe

Run these commands on Windows with absolute BUILD_DIRECTORY and APPLICATION
paths, using an application such as Browser that reaches WaitNextEvent. The probe
checks all four formats, resource-only rename, data/resource preservation,
duplicate creation and deletion. Use a new build directory for every run.

The separately deployed Netatalk patch and its AFP locking exception are
documented in tools/netatalk/README.md.

Existing coverage
-----------------

After configuring the Windows build, run in WSL with Windows interop enabled:

  bash system/tests/check-files.sh sidecar

The test compiles the actual fileDouble.c implementation into a Windows
executable, using CRT filesystem calls in place of ROMAW's host path
wrappers. It creates disposable fixtures under the workspace build directory.
No existing application files are modified.

Coverage: per-file and per-directory Netatalk, BasiliskII/SheepShaver and OS X
detection; compact 18-byte Finder records; raw resource forks at offset zero;
16-byte AppleDouble Finder records; unordered descriptors; truncated tables;
resource growth that preserves following metadata; and preserving the source
sidecar convention for a rename or move into a different-style directory;
moving/deleting companion pairs; and removing empty sidecar containers when
deleting a Macintosh directory.

The copy regression allocates a fresh AppleDouble resource fork, sets its EOF
again before writing, and reopens its descriptor. It checks the physical file
length, logical fork length and preserved seek position, then shrinks the fork.

For integration testing, launch a copy of a Macintosh application whose
metadata/resource forks are represented as .finf/name plus .rsrc/name, then
repeat with ._name AppleDouble. Confirm Finder type/creator, resource loading,
hidden sidecar entries, and no win_exception entries in stderr.txt.

The guest File Manager prefers existing companions in this order: valid ._name,
.AppleDouble/name, .finf/name (or .rsrc/name), then valid legacy %name.
New companions use the containing directory's convention: valid ._* AppleDouble
files or .DS_Store declare OS X, followed by .AppleDouble and then .finf/.rsrc.
Unmarked directories retain ROMAW's configured convention (OS X ._name by
default; -netatalk or -afpd selects .AppleDouble/name). These switches do not
convert existing files. Renames/moves preserve the individual file's format.
The [root overview](../../README.md#macintosh-sidecar-files) describes the
user-facing formats and command-line settings. Native Finder destination
selection and conversion are documented separately under
[Shared Macintosh storage layouts](../../finder/README.md#shared-macintosh-storage-layouts).

Use tools/convert-appledouble.py APPS --backup BACKUP to migrate Netatalk
and legacy percent sidecars to OS X naming. The tool validates descriptors,
backs up every source, rejects conflicting targets and verifies exact bytes
before removing sources. Directory .Parent metadata becomes the directory's
sibling ._name, including ._Apps beside the Apps directory. It does not
rename data forks or convert Basilisk raw companions. --replace-conflicts backs
up conflicting OS X targets before using the source metadata.
Run python3 system/tests/test-appledouble-conversion.py for migration fixtures.

Tiger interoperability: AppleDouble permits FinderInfo after a resource fork,
but OS X 10.4.11's GetAppleDoubleBulk crashes when FinderInfo is far beyond its
initial metadata buffer. This was reproduced on the user's PowerPC Mac for
Retro68's ROMAW Finder and the installed System file. A valid .DS_Store was
not the cause. Put FinderInfo next to the descriptor table and resource data last:

  python3 tools/normalize-appledouble.py SOURCE NEW_OUTPUT
  python3 system/tests/test-appledouble-normalization.py

The normalizer requires a new destination and emits exactly two descriptors:
FinderInfo (ID 9, offset 50, 32 bytes), then resource fork (ID 2, offset 82).
It validates all source extents/overlaps and preserves FinderInfo/resource data
(16-byte FinderInfo is padded to 32). Legacy-only records such as names, comments
and dates remain in the unchanged source/backup; they are not included in the
native OS X output. Extended FinderInfo/ATTR layouts are rejected, since their
embedded offsets need separate handling. The name-conversion tool above only
renames sidecars; normalize its output separately for native OS X writing.

ROMAW Finder's sidecar converter also supports modern FinderInfo/ATTR
records (including com.apple.provenance). Unlike the standalone normalizer,
it validates the bounded ATTR header, names and data extents, then relocates
absolute offsets when rewriting the AppleDouble table. Attribute values stream
without a 64-KiB payload limit and remain in OS X, percent and Netatalk output.
Compact Basilisk/SheepShaver metadata cannot represent these attributes; its
conversion retains the automatic recovery backup containing the original.
Native sidecars tests cover relocation, large attributes, malformed offsets
and compact-format recovery. The optional test argument accepts a copy of the
reported empty-resource-fork specimen with FinderInfo at offset 50.
Format reference: https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/bsd/vfs/vfs_xattr.c

Netatalk adEA index/value companions exposed as literal `::EA` filenames
(including SMB's U+F022 colon spelling) are auxiliary extended attributes,
not AppleDouble resource forks. Conversion keeps these files in place and
includes them in requested backups; it does not migrate them into ATTR.
The native sidecars fixture accepts `--netatalk-ea APPLEDOUBLE_FILE` to test
copies of the matching real-world index/value files without changing originals.

Close every local/remote client using the files before replacing sidecars,
including ResEdit/Classic over SMB. Keep verified original backups. Existing
foreign-format forks remain readable by ROMAW; new ._ forks use the native
two-entry layout, while Netatalk and Basilisk keep their own conventions.

The classic Finder CMake build normalizes generated percent sidecars before
staging. Both Apps/.DS_Store and Apps/System Folder/.DS_Store were valid and were
not the source of the crashes. Tiger opened both folders after normalization.

Read/copy success is insufficient: a writable Carbon Resource Manager save on
10.4.11 damaged both tested multi-entry layouts and the resource-first two-entry
layout. Only FinderInfo-first/resource-second survived. This reproduced in new,
disposable fixtures, so cached handles were not the only issue. The source of
Tiger's native two-entry xattr layout is Apple's xnu-792.25.20/bsd/vfs/vfs_xattr.c.

Run the write/reopen/remove fixture on Tiger's Python 2.3:

  ssh USER@MAC python - /path/to/disposable/SystemProbe < system/tests/tiger_resource_roundtrip.py

First create a disposable SystemProbe data fork and ._SystemProbe from a known
resource file. The script refuses other basenames and removes its test resource.
Then compare every original resource payload with the resulting fork, rather
than just counting resources. The System test retained all 35 types/297 resource
payloads exactly after saving, reopening and removing the added resource.

On 2026-10-23, 763 installed sidecars were backed up and normalized to the native
layout. FinderInfo/resource payloads matched exactly; full originals, including
legacy metadata, remain in build/tiger-native-sidecars-20261023-110240. The
previously damaged System resource was recovered from the earlier verified
backup. ROMAW Finder and all four installed accessory probes passed startup
and close tests afterward.

Resource-map EOF follow-up: UpdateResFile now truncates the resource fork at the
written map's end after a successful write. Previously, shrinking/compaction left
stale map/data bytes beyond the live map, which real Super ResEdit flagged.
The repaired System retains all 297 resource payloads; only unused trailing bytes
were removed. A trimmed test copy opened in Super ResEdit 2.1.3 without a damage
warning. check-toolbox.sh resource-map compiles the actual implementation natively
in WSL and covers truncation plus seek/write/truncate failures and invalid EOFs.
The same fixture also passed as a Windows executable. A staged ROMAW GDB
probe rewrote a deliberately oversized disposable fork, returned noErr, and
reduced its logical length exactly to mapOffset + mapLength.

Resource-fork deny opens
-----------------------

HFSDispatch selector 0x39 now opens a resource fork through OpenRFDeny instead
of returning paramErr. It shares the existing OpenDeny permission mapping and
parameter-block forwarding; this does not add stronger sharing-lock semantics.
Run `bash system/tests/check-files.sh open-deny` for intercepted native tests.
Active ROMAW tests do not build or launch the archived classic Finder.
